# =============================================================================
# Jamee — environment variables
# =============================================================================
#
# Requirement 17.2: every required variable name, a placeholder value, NO real secret.
# Requirement 13.7: only NEXT_PUBLIC_-prefixed values may reach the browser.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in. `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` says where each value
# comes from, step by step. `.env.example` itself is COMMITTED — it must never hold a live
# credential, a real project ref, or a real token.
#
# HOW FAILURE LOOKS. Every variable below is read through `serverEnv()` in `src/lib/env.ts`,
# which validates on FIRST USE and throws naming the missing variable. There is no silent
# default and no `process.env.X!` anywhere else in the codebase — an absent variable fails the
# request that needed it, loudly, rather than producing a wrong answer for a farmer.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Model identifiers, the Meta API version, header names, and
# platform limits are compile-time constants in `src/lib/config/verified.ts`, each annotated
# with the V-item in `docs/VERIFIED_VALUES.md` that resolved it. A model id in an env var is
# an unversioned, unreviewable change to Jamee's behaviour.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The complete set, at a glance
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The authority for this list is `serverEnvSchema` / `PUBLIC_ENV_VARS` / `OPTIONAL_SERVER_ENV_KEYS`
# in `src/lib/env.ts` and `src/lib/env.public.ts` — not this comment. `ALL_ENV_KEYS` is exported
# from `env.ts` so a completeness check (Property 30, task 28.5) compares against the schema
# rather than against prose.
#
#   PUBLIC (reach the browser, by design)
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL                REQUIRED
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY    REQUIRED — or its older name, below (V1)
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY           REQUIRED only if the publishable name is unset
#
#   SERVER ONLY — secrets
#     SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY               REQUIRED
#     GEMINI_API_KEY                          REQUIRED
#     GROQ_API_KEY                            REQUIRED
#     WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN                   REQUIRED
#     WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID                REQUIRED
#     WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN                   REQUIRED
#     WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET                     REQUIRED
#     CRON_SECRET                             REQUIRED (min 16 chars, V21)
#
#   SERVER ONLY — configuration, not a secret
#     WHATSAPP_API_VERSION                    OPTIONAL — overrides the V12-pinned version
#
#   TEST-ONLY, never set in production
#     JAMEE_TEST_DATABASE_URL                 OPTIONAL — absent means integration tests SKIP
#
# "REQUIRED" above means exactly what the Zod schema says: `serverEnv('NAME')` throws when the
# value is missing or empty. "OPTIONAL" means `optionalServerEnv('NAME')` returns `undefined`
# and the code has a documented behaviour for that case — leaving it unset is a supported
# configuration, not an omission.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — public (these DO reach the browser, by design)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Project URL. Public endpoint; RLS is what authorizes requests to it.
# Where: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Data API → Project URL.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# REQUIRED. The public client key. Set ONE of the two below.
# Where: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API keys.
#
# V1 records that current Supabase docs name this NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY, while
# older projects issue an anon JWT under NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY. Jamee accepts either,
# publishable first, so a project created on either side of the rename works unchanged.
#
# This key carries no privilege of its own — it is safe to publish.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_000000000000000000000000

# The older name, for a project issued before the rename. Uncomment this ONLY if the
# publishable name above is unset; `publicEnv()` prefers publishable when both are present.
# The real value is a JWT — three dot-separated base64url segments, like the shape below.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_ANON_KEY_PAYLOAD.REPLACE_WITH_SIGNATURE


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — server only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. SERVICE ROLE KEY. Bypasses row-level security entirely.
# Where: Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API keys → `service_role` (revealed on click).
# Same JWT shape as the anon key; the difference is the role claim inside it, which is why the
# two are easy to paste into the wrong variable — check which one you copied.
#
# Read only by `src/lib/supabase/admin.ts`, which begins with `import 'server-only'`,
# constructs its client inside a function, and throws when a browser global is present.
# `scripts/check-secret-scope.ts` fails CI if this name appears in any file containing
# 'use client'.
#
# Needed by: the WhatsApp adapter (a webhook carries no farmer session), the quota counter
# functions, and the retention sweep. Every service-role query in Jamee carries an explicit
# user_id predicate; one without it is a defect.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_SERVICE_ROLE_PAYLOAD.REPLACE_WITH_SIGNATURE


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI providers — server only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Google Gemini. Primary for `vision-classify` and `text-generate`.
# Where: Google AI Studio → Get API key.
#
# V6 records that the `@google/genai` client reads this exact name itself, which is why the
# variable is spelled this way and not renamed. `src/lib/gemini.ts` still passes it through
# `serverEnv()` so a missing key fails with env.ts's message rather than the SDK's.
# The model identifier is pinned in `src/lib/config/verified.ts` (V6), not here.
GEMINI_API_KEY=replace-with-your-gemini-api-key

# REQUIRED. Groq. Text fallback (V8), conditional vision fallback (V9), and transcription (V10).
# Where: Groq Console → API Keys. Real keys start `gsk_`.
#
# One key serves all three; a Groq outage therefore takes voice offline while image and chat
# continue on Gemini. That is a known single point of failure, not a defect.
GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_replace_with_your_groq_api_key

# NOTE — there is no VISION_FALLBACK_MODEL variable, and that is deliberate.
# The vision fallback is the compile-time constant `VISION_FALLBACK_MODEL` in
# `src/lib/config/verified.ts`, typed `string | null`. V9 records that Groq's vision models are
# *Preview* tier — "may be discontinued at short notice" — so setting that constant to `null`
# has to stay expressible: the gateway then returns a one-member chain and Requirement 14.8's
# single exhausted-chain branch keeps the farmer-visible behaviour identical. Running with NO
# vision fallback is therefore a supported configuration, not a misconfiguration — but it is a
# reviewable code edit with a V-item next to it, not an environment variable you can leave
# blank. Nothing to set here.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Meta WhatsApp Business Cloud API — server only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Bearer token for media download (V14) and sending replies (V15).
# Where: Meta App Dashboard → WhatsApp → API Setup. That page issues a short-lived token for
# testing; a deployment needs a long-lived System User token instead. The exact account
# procedure — and the token type to generate — is `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` step 5, not this file.
#
# V14 records that a media download failing with 404 is recovered by re-requesting the media
# URL and then, if it still fails, renewing this token — so token expiry is an operational
# event to plan for, not a one-time setup detail.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=replace-with-your-whatsapp-access-token

# REQUIRED. Business phone number id — the path segment of the send endpoint (V15).
# Where: Meta App Dashboard → WhatsApp → API Setup → "Phone number ID". A numeric id, NOT the
# display phone number.
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=000000000000000

# REQUIRED. Compared in constant time against `hub.verify_token` on the webhook GET challenge
# (Requirement 11.1, V13). You invent this value, then paste the same string into
# Meta App Dashboard → WhatsApp → Configuration → Webhook → Verify token.
WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=invent-a-long-random-string-and-paste-it-into-meta

# REQUIRED. App Secret. HMAC-SHA256 key for X-Hub-Signature-256, verified over the RAW request
# body before any JSON parsing (Requirement 11.3, V13).
# Where: Meta App Dashboard → App Settings → Basic → App secret (Show).
#
# U-V13: the digest's byte encoding (hex vs. base64) is not documented. `src/lib/whatsapp.ts`
# accepts either by comparing both candidates in constant time, rather than guessing one and
# rejecting every legitimate delivery.
WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=replace-with-your-meta-app-secret

# OPTIONAL. Overrides the V12-pinned Graph API version (currently v26.0 in
# `src/lib/config/verified.ts`). Leave unset unless Meta has deprecated the pinned version
# faster than a code change can ship — this is an escape hatch, not a value an operator is
# expected to supply. Unset is the normal configuration.
#
# It is server-side CONFIGURATION rather than a secret: an API version string carries no
# privilege, which is why `scripts/check-secret-scope.ts` deliberately leaves it out of
# SERVER_SECRET_NAMES while still keeping it off the browser side of the boundary.
# WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v26.0


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retention sweep — server only
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. Requirement 18.12's retention sweep secret. Minimum 16 characters, enforced by the
# schema; use a password generator.
# Where: you invent it, then set it as a project environment variable on the deployment
# platform (and in `.env.local` for a local manual run).
#
# Named CRON_SECRET because that is the name the platform injects: V21 records that Vercel
# automatically sends this variable's value as `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` on every
# cron invocation, so a differently named variable could not be used for automatic triggering
# at all. There is one secret here, not two.
#
# /api/internal/retention-sweep reads ONLY this header. A valid farmer session is explicitly
# not authorization for that route (Requirement 18.13).
CRON_SECRET=generate-a-random-string-of-at-least-16-characters


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests — optional, never set in production
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL. Integration tests run against a real Postgres. When this is absent the integration
# project is SKIPPED rather than failed, so `npm test` is green on a machine with no database.
# Read by `tests/integration/database.ts`, not by `serverEnv()` — it is test scaffolding and
# never ships.
# JAMEE_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54322/postgres
